WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.318 --> 00:00:03.720
(countdown in French)

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:07.220
(spacecraft engine roars)

00:00:10.396 --> 00:00:13.896
(scientist speaks French)

00:00:14.892 --> 00:00:17.520
- This spacecraft is embarking
on an eight year journey

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:20.640
to study Jupiter and three
of its largest moons.

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:23.730
The Jupiter Icy Moons
Explorer, also known as JUICE

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:26.880
is on a mission to characterize
what makes a world habitable

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:29.970
so it can sustain life
in our solar system.

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:33.090
- Are we really so special in the cosmos

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:36.210
that earth is the only place
that can support life forms?

00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:37.950
Here's a look at what JUICE will explore

00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:40.950
once it arrives at its destination
and what it might reveal

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:43.293
about the search for life in outer space.

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:47.490
The JUICE mission is led by
the European Space Agency

00:00:47.490 --> 00:00:50.310
partnering with other space
agencies across the world,

00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:53.790
including those in the
US, Japan, and Israel.

00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:56.190
On board, the spacecraft carries a suite

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:57.960
of 10 scientific instruments,

00:00:57.960 --> 00:00:59.820
making it the most powerful payload

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:02.280
flown to the outer solar system.

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:04.620
Those instruments include a camera system.

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:06.930
They include a radar
system that can penetrate

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:09.000
several miles into the surface.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:10.740
They're bringing a laser altimeter,

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:13.380
which will sort of help
them create a 3D map.

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:15.450
- Any spacecraft headed into outer space

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:18.720
will face challenges, from
navigating the asteroid belt

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:20.580
to a long roundabout journey.

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:22.470
But, JUICE was designed to withstand

00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:25.440
some of the universe's
extremes, including radiation,

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:26.940
and harsh temperatures.

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:28.680
On its journey, JUICE will encounter

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:31.590
nearly 500 degree
temperatures around Venus,

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:34.740
and close to minus 400 degree
temperatures near Jupiter.

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:36.480
When they build these instruments,

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:39.030
they not only need to
ensure that they can deliver

00:01:39.030 --> 00:01:40.950
on the science that's proposed, like,

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:43.110
they're sensitive enough
and calibrated enough

00:01:43.110 --> 00:01:45.720
to identify differences in
magnetic fields and things,

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:48.120
but also need to survive
the journey, right?

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:50.760
You can't send the plumber
to go fix the instruments.

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:52.800
JUICE which weighs about 10 tons,

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:56.520
must first complete flybys of
Earth, the Moon, and Venus,

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:59.520
so it can adjust its trajectory
and gain enough speed.

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:01.980
It was challenging to
design the spacecraft

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:05.430
because it has to take such
a, sort of, long journey

00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:06.600
to get to Jupiter.

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:10.170
By the time JUICE reaches its
destination in July of 2031,

00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:13.383
the spacecraft will have
traveled nearly 4 billion miles.

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:17.610
Sitting nearly 450 million
miles away from Earth,

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:20.400
Jupiter is the largest
planet in our solar system,

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:21.660
more than twice as massive

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:23.670
as all the other planets combined.

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:25.440
One of the NASA scientists

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:28.080
described Jupiter's planetary
neighborhood as, sort of like,

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:31.740
the Disneyland, Universal, and
Six-Flags of the solar system

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:32.910
all rolled into one.

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:35.100
The planet is mostly composed of gases,

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:38.322
like hydrogen and helium, and
has a powerful magnetic field

00:02:38.322 --> 00:02:41.460
that affects thousands
of objects in its orbit.

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:43.740
JUICE will, in part, study this atmosphere

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:45.750
to understand how the harsh environment

00:02:45.750 --> 00:02:48.660
shapes the conditions on
its surrounding moons.

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:51.000
Among nearly 100 moons orbiting Jupiter,

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:54.690
three of the largest Europa,
Ganymede, and Callisto,

00:02:54.690 --> 00:02:57.315
are all thought to possibly
harbor oceans of liquid water

00:02:57.315 --> 00:02:59.370
beneath their icy crusts.

00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:02.190
After previous missions shed
light on what lies beneath

00:03:02.190 --> 00:03:03.300
the surface.

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:05.190
JUICE's Radar instrument will, sort of,

00:03:05.190 --> 00:03:07.470
help scientists peer under the surface.

00:03:07.470 --> 00:03:09.988
It might help them understand
how thick the ice is,

00:03:09.988 --> 00:03:13.050
above what is potentially
a subsurface ocean.

00:03:13.050 --> 00:03:14.768
It might also help reveal

00:03:14.768 --> 00:03:16.830
whether or not this ocean is salty.

00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:19.710
Scientists will look for
liquid water, a heat source,

00:03:19.710 --> 00:03:21.960
and organic materials on these moons,

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:25.350
which are among the ingredients
needed to sustain life.

00:03:25.350 --> 00:03:26.730
It's likely gonna be microbes.

00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:28.290
We're we're not looking at aliens

00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:29.730
living under the ocean surface.

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:31.980
It could also be maybe
more advanced species,

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:35.580
sort of similar to what we
might find near deep sea vents

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:37.530
in our own oceans on Earth.

00:03:37.530 --> 00:03:40.290
- While JUICE can't detect
life, the mission is a step

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:42.690
toward identifying whether
these ingredients exist

00:03:42.690 --> 00:03:44.553
elsewhere in our solar system.

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:48.750
The JUICE mission is expected
to collect about four years

00:03:48.750 --> 00:03:51.420
of data, before ending in 2035,

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:54.090
but it's not the only
spacecraft observing Jupiter

00:03:54.090 --> 00:03:56.250
and it's surrounding moons.

00:03:56.250 --> 00:03:59.370
NASA is also sending a
mission to Europa next year,

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:01.050
named Europa Clipper.

00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:03.060
That mission will arrive in 2030,

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:05.970
and will observe the moon
at the same time as JUICE.

00:04:05.970 --> 00:04:08.700
Space Science has matured over
the past couple of decades,

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:10.440
and really honed in on Jupiter

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:13.020
and to look for those other life forms,

00:04:13.020 --> 00:04:15.360
and JUICE is a key requisite

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:18.840
to really making that objective a reality.

00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:23.430
There's no promise that we'll
detect life on these moons

00:04:23.430 --> 00:04:25.590
but if we don't look and see

00:04:25.590 --> 00:04:27.390
whether or not they're
potentially habitable

00:04:27.390 --> 00:04:29.344
we won't actually ever get the answer.

00:04:29.344 --> 00:04:32.677
(upbeat rhythmic music)

